So the passes are not vacuous.

INDEPENDENT SECOND DISCRIMINATOR (not in the audit): F&T p.7 also says that in 2D the bound is 'four times stronger than Ford and Roman' and '1 1/2 times weaker than the optimal one of Flanagan'. From (3.4) at n = 1 with A = 1/(2 pi), the bound is 1/(32 pi t0^2). Flanagan's (1/24 pi) INT f'^2/f on the Lorentzian is 1/(48 pi t0^2), computed symbolically. The ratio is exactly 3/2, and that comparison involves no Ford-Roman constant. Against Ford-Roman's 2D 1/(8 pi t0^2) the ratio is exactly 1/4. With A = 1/2 the same ratios are 3 pi/2 and pi/4. Caveat: the Flanagan and 2D Ford-Roman formulas were taken from memory and are NAMED-NOT-READ in this run. The check corroborates and is not the grade's basis.

